* START-DESCRIPTION:
*
*    deffun voc_guard(f) calling '!voc_guard'
*    if not(voc_guard(file.var)) then stop
*
* Owner's rulings 12 and 14, 25 Sep 2026: a DIRECT edit of the VOC needs the
* administrator password (ADMIN).  op_dio3.c refuses a user program's own
* write, delete or clear of a VOC in C; the verbs that edit a file the user
* names (ED, EDIT, COPY, COPYP, DELETE, CLEAR.FILE, and DELETE.FILE and RENAME
* when the VOC itself is the target) are $internal, which the C test lets
* through, so they ask this instead.
*
* IT LOOKS AT THE FILE THAT WAS OPENED, NOT AT THE NAME THAT WAS TYPED, so a
* Q-pointer to a VOC, or "voc" in any case, is caught.  A VOC is a DH (dynamic)
* file whose last path component is "voc" - the same rule as
* voc_write_refused() in C.  The dictionary, voc.dic, is not the VOC.
*
* Returns @true when the edit may go ahead: not a VOC, or ADMIN is on.  On
* @false it has already said why (12008).
*
* END-DESCRIPTION
*
* START-CODE

$internal
function voc_guard(f)
$catalog !voc_guard

$include keys.h
$include int$keys.h

   if kernel(K$ADMINISTRATOR, -1) then return @true

   if fileinfo(f, FL$TYPE) # FL$TYPE.DH then return @true

   p = downcase(convert(char(92), '/', fileinfo(f, FL$PATH)))
   if p[len(p), 1] = '/' then p = p[1, len(p) - 1]
   last = field(p, '/', dcount(p, '/'))

   if last # 'voc' then return @true

   display sysmsg(12008) ;* The VOC can only be changed after ADMIN
   return @false
end

* END-CODE
